---
/**
 * The control map: named compliance controls, and where each one actually
 * stands.
 *
 * Every status is *derived* from the ledger questions a control names, so this
 * page cannot claim more than `docs/roadmap.json` does. The disclaimer is at the
 * top rather than the bottom on purpose — a compliance page that leads with the
 * good news and buries the caveat is the exact genre this repository exists to
 * argue against.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StatusChip from '../components/StatusChip.astro';
import { inlineMarkdown } from '../components/inline';
import {
  frameworks,
  controlsInFramework,
  controlStatus,
  questionsFor,
  statusMeta,
  withBase,
  repoHref,
} from '../lib/roadmap';

const rows = frameworks.map((framework) => ({
  framework,
  controls: controlsInFramework(framework.id).map((control) => ({
    control,
    status: controlStatus(control),
    questions: questionsFor(control),
  })),
}));
---

<Base
  title="Control map"
  description="SOC 2, ISO 27001 and GDPR controls mapped to the questions this architecture answers — and to the test that proves each one. Technical prerequisites only."
  active="controls"
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 sm:py-20 lg:px-8">
    <header>
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-brand uppercase">Control map</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        What an auditor asks, and where this actually stands
      </h1>
    </header>

    {/* The disclaimer, first. */}
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-2xl border border-amber-500/30 bg-amber-500/[0.06] p-6">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold text-amber-700 dark:text-amber-300">Read this first</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <p>
          This is an engineering document describing what the code does, written in the vocabulary an
          auditor uses. It is <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">not</strong> an assertion of compliance,
          and it does not substitute for an audit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Roughly half of any real control is not in a repository at all: the policy, the training,
          the review cadence, the evidence that a human looked, and an auditor's judgement that all
          of it operated continuously over a six-to-twelve-month window. What follows covers the
          technical prerequisites — the part that is code, and can therefore be pointed at, tested,
          and shown not to have drifted.
        </p>
        <p>
          A control is scored by its <em>weakest</em> question, not its average. One open question
          means an auditor is going to ask, and rounding that up would defeat the point of writing it
          down.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {
      rows.map(({ framework, controls }) => (
        <section class="mt-16">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">{framework.name}</h2>
            <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted">{framework.revision}</p>
          </div>

          <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
            {controls.map(({ control, status, questions }) => (
              <li class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5">
                <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-2">
                  <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-balance">
                    <span class="font-mono text-brand">{control.id}</span>{' '}
                    <span class="text-fg">{control.name}</span>
                  </h3>
                  <StatusChip status={status} />
                </div>

                <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted italic">{control.asks}</p>

                <p
                  class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted"
                  set:html={inlineMarkdown(control.note)}
                />

                <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 border-t border-line pt-4">
                  <span class="text-xs text-muted">Bearing on it:</span>
                  {questions.map((question) => (
                    <span
                      class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-md border border-line bg-bg px-2 py-1 text-xs"
                      title={question.question}
                    >
                      <span aria-hidden="true">{statusMeta[question.status].icon}</span>
                      <span class="font-mono text-muted">{question.id}</span>
                      {question.proof ? (
                        <a
                          class="text-brand underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
                          href={repoHref(question.proof)}
                        >
                          proof
                        </a>
                      ) : null}
                    </span>
                  ))}
                </div>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))
    }

    <section class="mt-16 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-surface p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">What writing this map found</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        <p>
          Mapping controls to questions is a search for holes, and it found two. <strong
            class="font-semibold text-fg">Evidence of review</strong
          > had no question at all — logs existed, were complete, were protected, and nobody was reading
          them, which is the single most common finding in a real engagement. And <strong
            class="font-semibold text-fg">cryptography appears in exactly one place</strong
          >: the SHA-256 over the audit chain. Encryption at rest, in transit and key management belong
          to the deployment, and this repository says nothing about them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both are now rows on the ledger rather than absences nobody had named, which is the only
          useful thing a document like this does.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg px-3.5 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition hover:border-brand/40"
          href={withBase('/docs/compliance/')}
        >
          The same map, in the docs
        </a>
        <a
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-bg px-3.5 py-2 text-sm font-medium transition hover:border-brand/40"
          href={withBase('/docs/questions/')}
        >
          Every question, with its proof
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
